# A Redis client written in Heroes: it speaks RESP, the Redis serialization
# protocol, versions 2 and 3, over a TCP connection of `net` or a TLS
# connection of `tls`, and links no library. A command is a list of
# arguments, each sent as a bulk string, so a value never enters the text of
# a command and no argument needs quoting; a reply is a `Reply`, one case per
# type RESP writes. It logs in (`AUTH`, or `HELLO` with RESP3), selects a
# database, runs commands one at a time, in pipelines and in transactions
# (`MULTI` and `EXEC`, `WATCH`), publishes and subscribes, and holds a lock
# on one Redis server.
#
# **RESP3 by default**: `connect` asks for it with `HELLO 3`, which Redis 6 and
# later speak, and falls back to RESP2 where the server knows no `HELLO`.
# RESP3 tells a map, a set, a double, a boolean and a null apart, where RESP2
# answers arrays and strings; the readers (`text_of`, `int_of`,
# `entries_of`, ...) read either, so a program reads one way whatever the
# protocol. An attribute (`|`) is read and dropped.
#
# **TLS** begins from the first byte, as Redis serves it (`rediss://`); the
# server's certificate is checked for its authority and its host.
# `options` asks for TLS; a server on this machine that serves none, as
# Redis by default, is reached with `Security.plain`.
#
# It does not follow a cluster's redirections (`MOVED` and `ASK` fail
# `unsupported`), find a master through Sentinel, cache keys on the client
# (RESP3's invalidations are dropped), keep a pool of connections, or give a
# typed function to each of Redis's four hundred commands: `call` runs every
# one, and a handful of the commonest have their own. The ledger says so of
# each.
#
# ## The lock
#
# `try_lock` and `lock` take a lock on one Redis server: a key set only where
# it is absent (`SET key token NX PX ttl`), holding a token of 20 random
# bytes that only this holder knows, which expires after its time to live
# unless `renew` extends it; `unlock` deletes the key only where it still
# holds this holder's token, by a script the server runs whole. Each
# acquisition also takes a **fencing number**, which grows with every
# acquisition of the key.
#
# **What it does not promise**, which the program must weigh before it lets a
# lock guard anything that must not be done twice:
#
# - **A holder may lose the lock without knowing it.** A process paused past
#   the time to live (a long collection of garbage, a machine swapping, a
#   process stopped) wakes believing it holds a lock that expired and that
#   another took. Only the resource can refuse the late holder: let it
#   remember the highest fencing number it has seen and refuse a smaller one.
# - **One server is one point of failure.** Where Redis replicates to a
#   replica and fails over, the replica may not have the key yet, and a
#   second holder takes it.
# - **Redlock is not here.** Redis's documentation describes a lock taken on
#   a majority of five independent servers; it still assumes clocks that
#   drift little and processes that pause little, and still needs fencing
#   to be safe, as Martin Kleppmann's analysis of 2016 showed. Where
#   correctness depends on the lock, use a system built on consensus
#   (etcd, ZooKeeper) or the database's own locks; this lock serves where a
#   rare double run costs work and not truth.
#
# ## Failures
#
# A call fails with the codes below. An error the server replies keeps its
# word in brackets before its words, `[WRONGTYPE] Operation against a key
# holding the wrong kind of value`, and `error_code_of` reads the word back.
# The word chooses the code: `invalid` for `WRONGTYPE`, `EXECABORT` and a
# general `ERR` (a syntax error, a wrong number of arguments, a value that
# is no number); `permission` for `NOAUTH`, `NOPERM`, `WRONGPASS` and
# `READONLY`; `not_found` for `NOSCRIPT` and `NOGROUP`; `exists` for
# `BUSYGROUP`; `limit` for `OOM` and too many clients; `unsupported` for a
# command the server does not know and a cluster's `MOVED`, `ASK` and
# `CROSSSLOT`; and `failed` for every other, `LOADING` and `BUSY` among them.
# A connection lost, or bytes that are no RESP, end the client: every later
# call fails `closed`.

use bytes
use net
use numbers
use os
use redis/internal/failures
use redis/internal/lock_scripts
use redis/internal/pubsub
use redis/internal/resp
use redis/internal/script as server
use redis/internal/session
use redis/internal/stack
use strings
use time
use tls
use url

## Codes

# The code of an argument refused, a reply that is not of the kind asked
# for, bytes that are no RESP, and an error the server replies for a command
# it could not run as written: `call`, the readers, `decode`.
constant INVALID: str
    "invalid"

# The code of a key or a reply that holds nothing (`get`, the readers on a
# null), of a script or a group the server does not know, and of a lock
# that is no longer this holder's (`unlock`, `renew`).
constant NOT_FOUND: str
    "not_found"

# The code of a lock another holds (`try_lock`) and of a consumer group that
# exists already.
constant EXISTS: str
    "exists"

# The code of a login refused and of a command the user may not run.
constant PERMISSION: str
    "permission"

# The code of a client that is closed, or whose connection the server or the
# network closed while the call waited.
constant CLOSED: str
    "closed"

# The code of a call whose deadline passed, and of `lock` waiting past its
# time.
constant TIMEOUT: str
    "timeout"

# The code of a call that ended because the program was asked to stop.
constant STOPPED: str
    "stopped"

# The code of a server that has no more memory or connections, and of a
# reply past what the client reads.
constant LIMIT: str
    "limit"

# The code of a command the server does not know, a cluster's redirection,
# and a protocol the server does not speak.
constant UNSUPPORTED: str
    "unsupported"

# The code of every other error the server replies; the message carries its
# word and its words.
constant FAILED: str
    "failed"

## Connecting

# The port Redis listens on unless told another.
constant DEFAULT_PORT: i64
    6379

# How the connection to the server is secured.
variant Security
    # Nothing encrypted and nothing checked: whoever sits between the two
    # ends reads every byte, the password included. Redis's own default.
    plain
    # TLS from the first byte, the server's certificate checked for its
    # authority and its host: `rediss://`.
    checked
        # The PEM of the only authorities trusted; "" for the system's own
        # store.
        roots: str

# Where a server is and how to log in. `options` makes one; a program
# changes the fields it needs with `@`, or `parse_url` reads one from a URL.
record Options
    # The server's name or address, without brackets: `localhost`, `::1`.
    host: str
    # The server's TCP port, from 1 to 65535; `DEFAULT_PORT` unless told.
    port: i64
    # The user of Redis 6's access lists, "" for the `default` user.
    username: str
    # The password, "" where the server asks for none.
    password: str
    # The database selected, 0 to the server's count less one; 0 by default.
    database: i64
    # The protocol asked for: 3, RESP3, falling back to 2 where the server
    # knows no `HELLO`; or 2, RESP2 alone.
    protocol: i64
    # The name the server shows for this connection in `CLIENT LIST`, "" for
    # none.
    client_name: str
    # How long from when the connection is made every later wait on it may
    # last, failing `timeout`, as `net.connect` takes it, a part of a
    # millisecond counting as a whole one: zero ends at once every wait,
    # `time.max_duration()`, the default, sets none. `set_deadline` moves
    # it.
    deadline: time.Duration
    # How the connection is secured: `.checked(roots: "")` unless the
    # program asks for `.plain`.
    security: Security

# The options of a connection to `host` at `DEFAULT_PORT`, as the `default`
# user with no password, database 0, RESP3, no name and no deadline, over TLS
# checked against the system's own store.
#
# - `host`: the server's name or address, without brackets.
#
# See also `parse_url`, `connect`.
function options(host: str) -> Options
    return Options(
        host: host,
        port: DEFAULT_PORT,
        username: "",
        password: "",
        database: 0,
        protocol: 3,
        client_name: "",
        deadline: time.max_duration(),
        security: .checked(roots: "")
    )

# The options a `redis://` or `rediss://` URL names, as redis-py and
# go-redis read one: `redis://user:password@host:port/database`.
#
# - `text`: the URL. `redis://` is plain, `rediss://` TLS. Its user and
#   password are percent-decoded, `:password@` naming a password alone; a
#   host in brackets is an IPv6 address, `localhost` where none is written;
#   its path is the database's number, 0 where none is written. Its query
#   may name `protocol` (2 or 3) and `client_name`.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `url` is not such a URL, or names another key, a
# port outside 1 to 65535, a database that is no number of 0 or more, or a
# protocol other than 2 or 3, the message saying which part; `unsupported`
# for `unix://`, which `net` does not reach. See also `options`.
function parse_url(text: str) -> Options?
    u = match url.parse(text)
        .ok got => got
        .err e  => return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"\"{text}\" is no URL: {e.msg}")

    if u.scheme == "unix"
        return fail(code: UNSUPPORTED, msg: f"\"{text}\" names a Unix socket, which `net` does not reach")

    if u.scheme != "redis" && u.scheme != "rediss"
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"\"{text}\" opens with neither redis:// nor rediss://")

    host = if u.host == ""
        "localhost"
    else
        u.host
    opts @= options(host)

    if u.scheme == "redis"
        opts.security @ .plain
    opts.username @ u.user
    opts.password @ u.password

    if u.port != ""
        match url.port_of(u)
            .ok p =>
                if p < 1 || p > 65535
                    return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the port {u.port} of \"{text}\" is outside 1 to 65535")
                opts.port @ p
            .err _ => return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the port {u.port} of \"{text}\" is outside 1 to 65535")
    path = strings.trim_prefix(s: u.path, prefix: "/")

    if path != ""
        match numbers.parse_int(path)
            .ok n =>
                if n < 0
                    return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the database {path} of \"{text}\" is below 0")
                opts.database @ n
            .err _ => return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the path of \"{text}\" names no database's number")

    pairs = match url.parse_query(u.query)
        .ok got => got
        .err e  => return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the query of \"{text}\": {e.msg}")

    for pair in pairs
        if pair.key == "protocol"
            if pair.value != "2" && pair.value != "3"
                return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"protocol={pair.value} is neither 2 nor 3")
            opts.protocol @ numbers.parse_int(pair.value).must()
        else if pair.key == "client_name"
            opts.client_name @ pair.value
        else
            return fail(
                code: INVALID,
                msg: f"the key \"{pair.key}\" is none this client reads: it reads protocol and client_name"
            )

    return ok(opts)

# A connection to the server `opts` names, logged in, its protocol agreed and
# its database selected.
#
# - `opts`: where the server is and how to log in.
#
# The program owes the client a `close`. With `opts.protocol` 3 it sends
# `HELLO 3`, the login and the name with it; where the server knows no
# `HELLO` it speaks RESP2 and logs in with `AUTH`.
#
# Fails as `net.connect` fails where nothing answers, as `tls.connect` fails
# over TLS (`invalid` where the certificate is refused); `permission` where
# the server refuses the login, or asks for one and none was given;
# `invalid` where `opts.port`, `opts.database` or `opts.protocol` is out of
# range, `opts.deadline` below zero, or the database is past the server's
# count; `closed` where the
# server ends the connection. See also `close`, `options`.
function connect(opts: Options) -> Client?
    if opts.port < 1 || opts.port > 65535
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the port {opts.port} is outside 1 to 65535")

    if opts.database < 0
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the database {opts.database} is below 0")

    if opts.deadline.nanoseconds < 0
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the deadline {time.format_duration(opts.deadline)} is below zero")

    if opts.protocol != 2 && opts.protocol != 3
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the protocol {opts.protocol} is neither 2 nor 3")
    target = f"{opts.host}:{opts.port}"

    s = match opts.security
        .plain =>
            whole = opts.deadline.nanoseconds / 1_000_000

            plain_ms = if opts.deadline == time.max_duration()
                net.FOREVER
            else if opts.deadline.nanoseconds % 1_000_000 != 0
                whole + 1
            else
                whole
            conn = net.connect(opts.host, port: opts.port, deadline_ms: plain_ms)?
            session.over(out: conn.stream, input: conn.stream, link: .network, target: target)
        .checked k =>
            stream = tls.connect(host: opts.host, port: opts.port, deadline: opts.deadline, client: tls.Client(roots: k.roots, protocols: []))?.stream
            session.over(out: stream, input: stream, link: .network, target: target)

    c @= Client(session: s)

    match opts.security
        .plain => _ = 0
        .checked _ =>
            if opts.deadline != time.max_duration()
                match session.set_deadline(c.session, deadline: opts.deadline)
                    .ok _ => _ = 0
                    .err e =>
                        _ = session.close(@c.session).is_err()
                        return fail(code: e.code, msg: e.msg)

    setup: [[str]] @= []

    if opts.protocol == 3
        hello: [str] @= ["HELLO", "3"]

        if opts.password != ""
            user = if opts.username == ""
                "default"
            else
                opts.username
            hello @ hello.push("AUTH")
            hello @ hello.push(user)
            hello @ hello.push(opts.password)

        if opts.client_name != ""
            hello @ hello.push("SETNAME")
            hello @ hello.push(opts.client_name)
        said = call(@c, hello)

        match said
            .ok _ => c.session.protocol @ 3
            .err e =>
                if e.code != UNSUPPORTED || c.session.ended
                    _ = session.close(@c.session).is_err()
                    return fail(code: e.code, msg: e.msg)

    if c.session.protocol == 2
        if opts.password != "" && opts.username != ""
            setup @ setup.push(["AUTH", opts.username, opts.password])
        else if opts.password != ""
            setup @ setup.push(["AUTH", opts.password])

        if opts.client_name != ""
            setup @ setup.push(["CLIENT", "SETNAME", opts.client_name])

    if opts.database != 0
        setup @ setup.push(["SELECT", to_str(opts.database)])

    for words in setup
        match call(@c, words)
            .ok _ => _ = 0
            .err e =>
                _ = session.close(@c.session).is_err()
                return fail(code: e.code, msg: e.msg)

    return ok(c)

# Closes the connection and empties the client, so every later call through
# it fails `closed`.
#
# - `c`: the client, emptied.
#
# Fails `closed` where it is closed already. See also `connect`, `is_closed`.
function close(@c: Client) -> ()?
    return session.close(@c.session)

# Whether `close` has emptied `c`, or a lost connection or bytes that are no
# RESP have ended it.
#
# - `c`: the client asked about.
#
# See also `close`.
function is_closed(c: Client) -> bool
    return c.session.ended

# The protocol the server speaks with `c`: 3 for RESP3, 2 for RESP2.
#
# - `c`: the client asked about.
#
# See also `Options`.
function protocol_of(c: Client) -> i64
    return c.session.protocol

# Moves the deadline of `c`'s connection: every later wait on it, a
# `receive` among them, ends by `deadline` from now, failing `timeout`.
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `deadline`: how long from now; zero ends at once a wait,
#   `time.max_duration()` takes the deadline away, below zero fails
#   `invalid`.
#
# A wait that ends `timeout` leaves the client in step only where no reply
# was owed: after `receive`, never in the middle of a command's reply, which
# ends the client. Fails `closed` where `c` is closed. See also `receive`.
function set_deadline(c: Client, deadline: time.Duration) -> ()?
    return session.set_deadline(c.session, deadline: deadline)

## The client

# A connection to a Redis server, which `connect` makes and `close` ends. Its
# one field is the conversation's private state: the socket, the bytes read
# and not yet given out, the protocol, the channels subscribed to and the
# messages that came while a reply was awaited. Every call works on the
# caller's own place, through `@`. A program keeps one place per connection.
record Client
    # The conversation's state, which only this package reads and writes.
    session: session.Session

## Replies

# A reply, as RESP writes one: each case one of its types. RESP2 writes the
# first six; RESP3 all of them.
variant Reply
    # A simple string, `+OK`.
    simple
        # Its text.
        v: str
    # An error, `-ERR ...`, or a blob error, `!`.
    error
        # The word in capitals that opens it, `ERR`, `WRONGTYPE`; "" where
        # it opens with none.
        code: str
        # The server's words after that word.
        message: str
    # An integer, `:1000`.
    integer
        # The number.
        v: i64
    # A bulk string, `$5\r\nhello`: any bytes.
    bulk
        # The bytes, which may be empty.
        v: [u8]
    # Nothing: RESP3's null, `_`, and RESP2's null bulk string and null
    # array, `$-1` and `*-1`.
    null_value
    # An array, `*`: its elements in order.
    array
        # The elements.
        items: [Reply]
    # RESP3's map, `%`: its entries in the order the server wrote them.
    mapping
        # The entries.
        entries: [Entry]
    # RESP3's set, `~`: its elements, none twice.
    set
        # The elements, in the server's order.
        items: [Reply]
    # RESP3's double, `,1.23`, `,inf`, `,nan`.
    double
        # The number.
        v: f64
    # RESP3's boolean, `#t` or `#f`.
    boolean
        # The truth value.
        v: bool
    # RESP3's big number, `(`: an integer of any size.
    big_number
        # Its digits, a `-` before them where it is negative.
        v: str
    # RESP3's verbatim string, `=`: text with its format.
    verbatim
        # The format's three characters: `txt` for plain text, `mkd` for
        # Markdown.
        format: str
        # The text.
        v: str
    # RESP3's push, `>`: a message out of band, as a channel's message.
    pushed
        # The elements; the first names the push's kind.
        items: [Reply]

# One entry of a map.
record Entry
    # The key.
    key: Reply
    # Its value.
    value: Reply

# A reply read from bytes, and where the bytes after it begin.
record Decoded
    # The reply.
    reply: Reply
    # The byte index of the first byte after it.
    next: i64

# The one reply `data` holds, with nothing after it.
#
# - `data`: the bytes of a reply as RESP2 or RESP3 writes it.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `data` is no reply, ends inside one, holds bytes
# after it, or nests past 128 aggregates; `limit` where a string or a line
# passes the client's bounds; `unsupported` for a streamed string or
# aggregate. See also `decode_next`.
function decode(data: [u8]) -> Reply?
    d = decode_next(data, from: 0)?

    if d.next != data.len()
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the reply ends at byte {d.next} of {data.len()}")
    return ok(d.reply)

# The reply that begins at byte `from` of `data`, and where the next begins.
# An attribute before it is read and dropped.
#
# - `data`: bytes holding replies one after another.
# - `from`: where the reply begins, 0 to `data`'s length.
#
# It holds its own stack, so a reply nested deep never deepens the
# program's. Fails as `decode` does, but for bytes after the reply.
function decode_next(data: [u8], from: i64) -> Decoded?
    levels: [[Reply]] @= []
    kinds: [u8] @= []
    wants: [i64] @= []
    depth: i64 @= 0
    at: i64 @= from

    while true
        scanned = resp.scan(data, at: at)?

        if !scanned.complete
            return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the bytes end inside a reply that begins at byte {from}")
        t = scanned.token
        at @ t.next

        if resp.is_open(t)
            if depth >= session.MOST_DEPTH
                return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the reply nests past {session.MOST_DEPTH} levels")

            if depth == levels.len()
                levels @ levels.push([])
                kinds @ kinds.push(0)
                wants @ wants.push(0)
            levels[depth] @ []
            kinds[depth] @ t.kind
            wants[depth] @ resp.elements_of(t)
            depth @ depth + 1
        else
            value: Reply @= .null_value
            built: u8 @= 0
            items: [Reply] @= []

            if t.count == -1 || t.kind == '_'
                value @ .null_value
            else if t.kind == '+'
                value @ .simple(v: resp.text_of(t.data)?)
            else if t.kind == '-' || t.kind == '!'
                said = failures.said(bytes.to_text_lossy(t.data))
                value @ .error(code: said.prefix, message: said.words)
            else if t.kind == ':'
                value @ .integer(v: resp.number(t.data, from: 0, to: t.data.len())?)
            else if t.kind == '$'
                value @ .bulk(v: t.data)
            else if t.kind == '#'
                value @ .boolean(v: t.data[0] == 't')
            else if t.kind == ','
                value @ .double(v: resp.double_of(resp.text_of(t.data)?)?)
            else if t.kind == '('
                value @ .big_number(v: resp.big_number_of(t.data)?)
            else if t.kind == '='
                whole = resp.text_of(t.data)?
                value @ .verbatim(format: whole.slice(from: 0, to: 3), v: whole.slice(from: 4, to: whole.len()))
            else
                built @ t.kind
            climbing: bool @= true

            while climbing
                if built == '|'
                    climbing @ false
                else
                    if built == '%'
                        entries: [Entry] @= []
                        i: i64 @= 0

                        while i + 1 < items.len()
                            entries @ entries.push(Entry(key: items[i], value: items[i + 1]))
                            i @ i + 2

                        value @ .mapping(entries: entries)
                    else if built == '~'
                        value @ .set(items: items)
                    else if built == '>'
                        value @ .pushed(items: items)
                    else if built != 0
                        value @ .array(items: items)

                    if depth == 0
                        return ok(Decoded(reply: value, next: at))
                    stack.put(@levels[depth - 1], item: value)
                    wants[depth - 1] @ wants[depth - 1] - 1

                    if wants[depth - 1] > 0
                        climbing @ false
                    else
                        depth @ depth - 1
                        items @ levels[depth]
                        levels[depth] @ []
                        built @ kinds[depth]

# The bytes of the command `words` as RESP writes one: an array of bulk
# strings, which every version of Redis reads.
#
# - `words`: the command's name, then its arguments, each any bytes; the
#   name in any case.
#
# See also `call_bytes`.
function encode_command(words: [[u8]]) -> [u8]
    return resp.command(words)

# `r` itself where it is no error, else the failure the error tells: its
# code chosen by its word, its message its word in brackets and its words.
#
# - `r`: a reply, as `read_reply` and `pipeline` answer one.
#
# Fails where `r` is an error reply, with the code its word chooses (the
# module's comment): `invalid`, `permission`, `not_found`, `exists`,
# `limit`, `unsupported` or `failed`. See also `error_code_of`.
function checked(r: Reply) -> Reply?
    match r
        .error e =>
            said = failures.Said(prefix: e.code, words: e.message)
            return fail(code: failures.code_of(prefix: e.code, words: e.message), msg: failures.message_of(said))
        .simple _ | .integer _ | .bulk _ | .null_value | .array _ | .mapping _ | .set _ | .double _ | .boolean _ | .big_number _ | .verbatim _ | .pushed _ => return ok(
            r
        )

# The word an error reply's failure was told with: `WRONGTYPE` in
# `[WRONGTYPE] Operation against a key holding the wrong kind of value`.
#
# - `msg`: a failure's message, as `e.msg` holds it.
#
# Fails `not_found` where `msg` does not open with a word in brackets. See
# also `checked`.
function error_code_of(msg: str) -> str?
    if !strings.has_prefix(s: msg, prefix: "[")
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"\"{msg}\" opens with no word in brackets")

    match strings.split_once(s: msg.slice(from: 1, to: msg.len()), sep: "] ")
        .ok h =>
            if failures.is_capitals(h.before)
                return ok(h.before)
            return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"\"{msg}\" opens with no word in brackets")
        .err _ => return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"\"{msg}\" opens with no word in brackets")

# The name of the case `r` is, as a failure's message names it: `a simple
# string`, `an integer`, `a map`.
#
# - `r`: the reply.
function kind_of(r: Reply) -> str
    return match r
        .simple _     => "a simple string"
        .error _      => "an error"
        .integer _    => "an integer"
        .bulk _       => "a bulk string"
        .null_value   => "null"
        .array _      => "an array"
        .mapping _    => "a map"
        .set _        => "a set"
        .double _     => "a double"
        .boolean _    => "a boolean"
        .big_number _ => "a big number"
        .verbatim _   => "a verbatim string"
        .pushed _     => "a push"

# Whether `r` is null: RESP3's null, or RESP2's null bulk string or null
# array, as `GET` of a key that is not there answers.
#
# - `r`: the reply.
function is_null(r: Reply) -> bool
    return r == .null_value

# The text `r` holds: a simple string, a bulk string's bytes, a verbatim
# string's text, a big number's digits.
#
# - `r`: the reply.
#
# Fails `not_found` where `r` is null, `invalid` where it is of another kind
# or a bulk string that is not UTF-8, and with its own failure where it is
# an error. See also `bytes_of`, `texts_of`.
function text_of(r: Reply) -> str?
    _ = checked(r)?

    match r
        .simple s     => return ok(s.v)
        .bulk b       => return resp.text_of(b.v)
        .verbatim v   => return ok(v.v)
        .big_number n => return ok(n.v)
        .error _ | .integer _ | .null_value | .array _ | .mapping _ | .set _ | .double _ | .boolean _ | .pushed _ => return failures.not_kind(
            null_reply: is_null(r),
            kind: kind_of(r),
            wanted: "text"
        )

# The bytes `r` holds: a bulk string's, or the text of a simple string, a
# verbatim string or a big number.
#
# - `r`: the reply.
#
# Fails as `text_of` does, but never for bytes that are not UTF-8.
function bytes_of(r: Reply) -> [u8]?
    _ = checked(r)?

    match r
        .bulk b       => return ok(b.v)
        .simple s     => return ok(bytes.from_text(s.v))
        .verbatim v   => return ok(bytes.from_text(v.v))
        .big_number n => return ok(bytes.from_text(n.v))
        .error _ | .integer _ | .null_value | .array _ | .mapping _ | .set _ | .double _ | .boolean _ | .pushed _ => return failures.not_kind(
            null_reply: is_null(r),
            kind: kind_of(r),
            wanted: "bytes"
        )

# The integer `r` holds: an integer, or a string of decimal digits, as
# RESP2 answers `HGET` of a counter.
#
# - `r`: the reply.
#
# Fails `not_found` where `r` is null, `invalid` where it is of another kind
# or writes no number of an `i64`, and with its own failure where it is an
# error.
function int_of(r: Reply) -> i64?
    _ = checked(r)?

    match r
        .integer n => return ok(n.v)
        .simple _ | .bulk _ =>
            data = bytes_of(r)?
            return resp.number(data, from: 0, to: data.len())
        .error _ | .null_value | .array _ | .mapping _ | .set _ | .double _ | .boolean _ | .big_number _ | .verbatim _ | .pushed _ => return failures.not_kind(
            null_reply: is_null(r),
            kind: kind_of(r),
            wanted: "an integer"
        )

# The number `r` holds: a double, an integer, or a string that writes one,
# as RESP2 answers `ZSCORE` and `INCRBYFLOAT`, `inf` and `-inf` included.
#
# - `r`: the reply.
#
# Fails as `int_of` does.
function float_of(r: Reply) -> f64?
    _ = checked(r)?

    match r
        .double d           => return ok(d.v)
        .integer n          => return ok(to_f64(n.v))
        .simple _ | .bulk _ => return resp.double_of(text_of(r)?)
        .error _ | .null_value | .array _ | .mapping _ | .set _ | .boolean _ | .big_number _ | .verbatim _ | .pushed _ => return failures.not_kind(
            null_reply: is_null(r),
            kind: kind_of(r),
            wanted: "a number"
        )

# The truth `r` holds: a boolean, or the integer 0 or 1, as RESP2 answers
# `EXPIRE` and `SISMEMBER`.
#
# - `r`: the reply.
#
# Fails as `int_of` does, and `invalid` for an integer other than 0
# and 1.
function bool_of(r: Reply) -> bool?
    _ = checked(r)?

    match r
        .boolean b => return ok(b.v)
        .integer n =>
            if n.v == 0 || n.v == 1
                return ok(n.v == 1)
            return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the reply is the integer {n.v} where 0 or 1 was asked for")
        .simple _ | .error _ | .bulk _ | .null_value | .array _ | .mapping _ | .set _ | .double _ | .big_number _ | .verbatim _ | .pushed _ => return failures.not_kind(
            null_reply: is_null(r),
            kind: kind_of(r),
            wanted: "a boolean"
        )

# The elements `r` holds: an array's, a set's or a push's, in order.
#
# - `r`: the reply.
#
# Fails `not_found` where `r` is null, as RESP2 answers an array that is
# none; `invalid` where it is of another kind; with its own failure where it
# is an error. See also `texts_of`, `entries_of`.
function list_of(r: Reply) -> [Reply]?
    _ = checked(r)?

    match r
        .array a  => return ok(a.items)
        .set s    => return ok(s.items)
        .pushed p => return ok(p.items)
        .simple _ | .error _ | .integer _ | .bulk _ | .null_value | .mapping _ | .double _ | .boolean _ | .big_number _ | .verbatim _ => return failures.not_kind(
            null_reply: is_null(r),
            kind: kind_of(r),
            wanted: "a list"
        )

# The text of each element `r` holds, as `list_of` and `text_of` read them:
# what `KEYS`, `SMEMBERS` and `LRANGE` answer.
#
# - `r`: the reply.
#
# Fails as `list_of` fails, and as `text_of` fails on an element, a null one
# (`MGET` of a key that is not there) failing `not_found`.
function texts_of(r: Reply) -> [str]?
    out: [str] @= []

    for item in list_of(r)?
        out @ out.push(text_of(item)?)

    return ok(out)

# The entries `r` holds: a map's, or the keys and values in turn of an array
# or a set of even length, as RESP2 answers `HGETALL` and `CONFIG GET`.
#
# - `r`: the reply.
#
# Fails `invalid` where an array holds an odd count, and as `list_of` does.
function entries_of(r: Reply) -> [Entry]?
    _ = checked(r)?

    match r
        .mapping m => return ok(m.entries)
        .array _ | .set _ =>
            items = list_of(r)?

            if items.len() % 2 != 0
                return fail(
                    code: INVALID,
                    msg: f"the reply holds {items.len()} elements, an odd count, where keys and values were asked for"
                )
            out: [Entry] @= []
            i: i64 @= 0

            while i < items.len()
                out @ out.push(Entry(key: items[i], value: items[i + 1]))
                i @ i + 2

            return ok(out)
        .simple _ | .error _ | .integer _ | .bulk _ | .null_value | .double _ | .boolean _ | .big_number _ | .verbatim _ | .pushed _ => return failures.not_kind(
            null_reply: is_null(r),
            kind: kind_of(r),
            wanted: "a map"
        )

## Commands

# Writes the command `words` to the server without reading its reply, which
# `read_reply` reads: a pipeline written by hand.
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `words`: the command's name, then its arguments, each any bytes; at
#   least the name.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `words` is empty, and `closed` where the client is.
# See also `read_reply`, `pipeline`.
function send(@c: Client, words: [[u8]]) -> ()?
    if words.len() == 0
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: "a command needs at least its name")
    return session.send(@c.session, data: resp.command(words))

# The next reply the server sends, waiting until it comes; an error reply
# is answered as `.error`, not a failure. A RESP3 push that comes first, a
# message of a channel subscribed to, is kept for `receive`, at most 65,536
# of them unread, past which the oldest are lost; any other push, as an
# invalidation of the client's cache, is dropped.
#
# - `c`: the client.
#
# Fails `closed` where the connection ends, `timeout` where the deadline
# passes, and `invalid` where the bytes are no RESP, each ending the client.
# See also `send`, `checked`.
function read_reply(@c: Client) -> Reply?
    while true
        frame = session.frame(@c.session)?

        if c.session.protocol == 3 && frame[0] == '>'
            seen = pubsub.classify(frame)?

            if pubsub.is_message(seen.kind) || pubsub.is_confirmation(seen.kind)
                session.keep(@c.session, reply: frame)
        else
            match decode(frame)
                .ok r => return ok(r)
                .err e =>
                    c.session.ended @ true
                    return fail(code: e.code, msg: f"{c.session.target} sent {e.msg}")

# Runs the command `words` and answers its reply.
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `words`: the command's name, then its arguments: `["SET", "k", "v"]`.
#   Each is sent as it is, so none needs quoting.
#
# Fails with the error the server replies (the module's comment); `invalid`
# where `words` is empty, or where the client is subscribed over RESP2 and
# the command is not one a subscribed connection runs; `closed` where the
# connection is lost. See also `call_bytes`, `pipeline`.
function call(@c: Client, words: [str]) -> Reply?
    data: [[u8]] @= []

    for a in words
        data @ data.push(bytes.from_text(a))

    return call_bytes(@c, data)

# Runs the command `words`, arguments of any bytes, and answers its reply.
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `words`: the command's name, then its arguments, each any bytes.
#
# Fails as `call` does. See also `call`.
function call_bytes(@c: Client, words: [[u8]]) -> Reply?
    if words.len() > 0 && c.session.protocol == 2 && c.session.channels.len() + c.session.patterns.len() > 0
        name = strings.to_upper(bytes.to_text_lossy(words[0]))

        if !(name == "SUBSCRIBE" || name == "PSUBSCRIBE" || name == "UNSUBSCRIBE" || name == "PUNSUBSCRIBE" || name == "PING" || name == "RESET")
            return fail(
                code: INVALID,
                msg: f"the client is subscribed over RESP2, where {name} does not run; RESP3 runs every command"
            )
    send(@c, words)?
    return checked(read_reply(@c)?)

# The text `key` holds (`GET`).
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `key`: the key.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the key is not there, `invalid` where it holds
# bytes that are not UTF-8 (`call_bytes` reads them), and as `call` does,
# `invalid` where the key holds no string.
function get(@c: Client, key: str) -> str?
    return text_of(call(@c, ["GET", key])?)

# Sets `key` to `value`, with no time to live (`SET`).
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `key`: the key.
# - `value`: the text.
#
# Fails as `call` does. See also `set_for`.
function set(@c: Client, key: str, value: str) -> ()?
    _ = call(@c, ["SET", key, value])?
    return ok()

# Sets `key` to `value` for `ttl`, after which it is gone
# (`SET key value PX ttl`).
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `key`: the key.
# - `value`: the text.
# - `ttl`: how long it lives, 1 ms or more, sent in whole milliseconds, a
#   part of one counting as a whole one.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `ttl` is below 1 ms, and as `call` does.
function set_for(@c: Client, key: str, value: str, ttl: time.Duration) -> ()?
    ttl_ms = session.whole_ms(ttl)

    if ttl_ms < 1
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the time to live {time.format_duration(ttl)} is below 1 millisecond")
    _ = call(@c, ["SET", key, value, "PX", to_str(ttl_ms)])?
    return ok()

# Deletes each of `names`, and answers how many were there (`DEL`).
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `names`: the keys, at least one.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `names` is empty, and as `call` does.
function delete(@c: Client, names: [str]) -> i64?
    if names.len() == 0
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: "DEL needs at least one key")
    words: [str] @= ["DEL"]

    for k in names
        words @ words.push(k)

    return int_of(call(@c, words)?)

# Adds `by` to the integer `key` holds, 0 where it is not there, and answers
# the sum (`INCRBY`).
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `key`: the key.
# - `by`: the amount, negative to subtract.
#
# Fails `invalid` where the key holds no integer or the sum passes an
# `i64`, and as `call` does.
function increment(@c: Client, key: str, by: i64) -> i64?
    return int_of(call(@c, ["INCRBY", key, to_str(by)])?)

## Pipelines and transactions

# Runs every command of `commands` in one round trip, all written before any
# reply is read, and answers their replies in order. An error reply is
# answered as `.error` in its place; `checked` turns one into a failure.
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `commands`: the commands, each a name and its arguments; [] answers [].
#
# The commands are not a transaction: another client's commands may run
# between them. Fails `invalid` where a command is empty, `closed` where the
# connection is lost. See also `transaction`, `send`.
function pipeline(@c: Client, commands: [[str]]) -> [Reply]?
    data: [u8] @= []

    for words in commands
        if words.len() == 0
            return fail(code: INVALID, msg: "a command of the pipeline is empty")
        parts: [[u8]] @= []

        for a in words
            parts @ parts.push(bytes.from_text(a))

        session.append(out: @data, data: resp.command(parts))

    session.send(@c.session, data: data)?
    out: [Reply] @= []

    for _ in commands
        out @ out.push(read_reply(@c)?)

    return ok(out)

# What a transaction answered.
record Transaction
    # Whether it ran: false where a key `watch` named changed before `EXEC`,
    # and then no command ran.
    committed: bool
    # Each command's reply in order where it ran, an error in its place
    # where one failed as it ran; none where it did not run.
    replies: [Reply]

# Runs every command of `commands` as one transaction (`MULTI`, the
# commands, `EXEC`): no other client's command runs between them, and none
# of them runs where a key `watch` named has changed.
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `commands`: the commands, each a name and its arguments.
#
# A command that fails as it runs fails alone: the others run, and its
# reply is an `.error` in its place, since Redis does not roll back. Fails
# `invalid` where a command is empty, or where the server refuses one before
# it runs (an unknown command, a wrong number of arguments), when none runs;
# as `call` fails where `MULTI` is refused. See also `watch`.
function transaction(@c: Client, commands: [[str]]) -> Transaction?
    for words in commands
        if words.len() == 0
            return fail(code: INVALID, msg: "a command of the transaction is empty")

    _ = call(@c, ["MULTI"])?
    queued: [[str]] @= commands
    queued @ queued.push(["EXEC"])
    replies = pipeline(@c, queued)?
    first_error: Reply @= .null_value

    for r in replies.slice(from: 0, to: commands.len())
        match r
            .error _ =>
                if first_error == .null_value
                    first_error @ r
            .simple _ | .integer _ | .bulk _ | .null_value | .array _ | .mapping _ | .set _ | .double _ | .boolean _ | .big_number _ | .verbatim _ | .pushed _ => _ = 0

    done = replies[commands.len()]

    match done
        .error e =>
            if first_error != .null_value
                _ = checked(first_error)?
            return fail(
                code: failures.code_of(prefix: e.code, words: e.message),
                msg: failures.message_of(failures.Said(prefix: e.code, words: e.message))
            )
        .null_value => return ok(Transaction(committed: false, replies: []))
        .simple _ | .integer _ | .bulk _ | .array _ | .mapping _ | .set _ | .double _ | .boolean _ | .big_number _ | .verbatim _ | .pushed _ => return ok(
            Transaction(committed: true, replies: list_of(done)?)
        )

# Watches `names`: the next `transaction` on this client runs none of its
# commands where another client has changed one of them since (`WATCH`).
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `names`: the keys, at least one.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `names` is empty, and as `call` does. See also
# `unwatch`, `transaction`.
function watch(@c: Client, names: [str]) -> ()?
    if names.len() == 0
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: "WATCH needs at least one key")
    words: [str] @= ["WATCH"]

    for k in names
        words @ words.push(k)

    _ = call(@c, words)?
    return ok()

# Forgets the keys `watch` named (`UNWATCH`); a transaction does the same
# when it ends.
#
# - `c`: the client.
#
# Fails as `call` does. See also `watch`.
function unwatch(@c: Client) -> ()?
    _ = call(@c, ["UNWATCH"])?
    return ok()

## Publish and subscribe

# A message published to a channel the client is subscribed to.
record Message
    # How it came: `message` for a channel subscribed to by name,
    # `pmessage` by a pattern, `smessage` by a shard channel.
    kind: str
    # The pattern it matched, "" but for `pmessage`.
    pattern: str
    # The channel it was published to.
    channel: str
    # The message's bytes.
    payload: [u8]

# Posts `message` to `channel`, and answers how many clients received it
# (`PUBLISH`).
#
# - `c`: the client; a client subscribed over RESP2 cannot publish.
# - `channel`: the channel.
# - `message`: the text posted.
#
# Fails as `call` does. See also `subscribe`.
function publish(@c: Client, channel: str, message: str) -> i64?
    return int_of(call(@c, ["PUBLISH", channel, message])?)

# Subscribes to `channels`, waiting until the server confirms each
# (`SUBSCRIBE`); `receive` then reads their messages.
#
# - `c`: the client. Over RESP2 a subscribed client runs no command but
#   these four and `PING`; over RESP3 it runs every one.
# - `channels`: the channels' names, at least one.
#
# Messages that come before the last confirmation are kept for `receive`.
# Fails `invalid` where `channels` is empty, and as `call` does. See also
# `psubscribe`, `unsubscribe`, `receive`.
function subscribe(@c: Client, channels: [str]) -> ()?
    return pubsub.subscription(@c.session, name: "SUBSCRIBE", targets: channels, confirming: "subscribe")

# Subscribes to every channel whose name matches one of `patterns`, as
# `subscribe` does by name (`PSUBSCRIBE`).
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `patterns`: glob patterns, at least one: `news.*`.
#
# Fails as `subscribe` does. See also `punsubscribe`.
function psubscribe(@c: Client, patterns: [str]) -> ()?
    return pubsub.subscription(@c.session, name: "PSUBSCRIBE", targets: patterns, confirming: "psubscribe")

# Ends the subscriptions to `channels`, or to every channel where it is
# empty, waiting until the server confirms each (`UNSUBSCRIBE`).
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `channels`: the channels' names; [] for all.
#
# Fails as `call` does. See also `subscribe`.
function unsubscribe(@c: Client, channels: [str]) -> ()?
    return pubsub.subscription(@c.session, name: "UNSUBSCRIBE", targets: channels, confirming: "unsubscribe")

# Ends the subscriptions to `patterns`, or to every pattern where it is
# empty (`PUNSUBSCRIBE`).
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `patterns`: the patterns; [] for all.
#
# Fails as `call` does. See also `psubscribe`.
function punsubscribe(@c: Client, patterns: [str]) -> ()?
    return pubsub.subscription(@c.session, name: "PUNSUBSCRIBE", targets: patterns, confirming: "punsubscribe")

# The message a reply carries: a push of RESP3, or an array of RESP2 read
# while subscribed, whose first element names it `message`, `pmessage` or
# `smessage`.
#
# - `r`: the reply.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `r` carries no message.
function message_of(r: Reply) -> Message?
    items = match r
        .array a  => a.items
        .pushed p => p.items
        .simple _ | .error _ | .integer _ | .bulk _ | .null_value | .mapping _ | .set _ | .double _ | .boolean _ | .big_number _ | .verbatim _ => return fail(
            code: INVALID,
            msg: f"the reply is {kind_of(r)}, which carries no message"
        )

    if items.len() == 3 || items.len() == 4
        kind = strings.to_lower(text_of(items[0]).default(""))

        if (kind == "message" || kind == "smessage") && items.len() == 3
            return ok(Message(kind: kind, pattern: "", channel: text_of(items[1])?, payload: bytes_of(items[2])?))

        if kind == "pmessage" && items.len() == 4
            return ok(
                Message(kind: kind, pattern: text_of(items[1])?, channel: text_of(items[2])?, payload: bytes_of(items[3])?)
            )
    return fail(code: INVALID, msg: "the reply carries no message")

# The next message of a channel the client is subscribed to, waiting until
# one comes; one kept while a reply was awaited comes first.
#
# - `c`: the client.
#
# A confirmation and a `PING`'s answer that come meanwhile are read and
# passed. `set_deadline` bounds the wait. Fails `invalid` where the client is
# subscribed to nothing and keeps no message, so no message could come;
# `timeout` where the deadline passes; `closed` where the connection is lost.
# See also `subscribe`, `set_deadline`.
function receive(@c: Client) -> Message?
    while true
        frame: [u8] @= []

        if session.pending_count(c.session) > 0
            frame @ session.take(@c.session)?
        else
            if c.session.channels.len() + c.session.patterns.len() == 0
                return fail(code: INVALID, msg: "the client is subscribed to nothing, so no message can come")
            frame @ session.frame(@c.session)?
        seen = pubsub.classify(frame)?

        if pubsub.is_message(seen.kind)
            return message_of(decode(frame)?)

        if pubsub.is_confirmation(seen.kind)
            session.confirm(@c.session, kind: seen.kind, target: seen.target)

## Locks

# A lock `try_lock` or `lock` took, which `unlock` gives back.
record Lock
    # The key that holds it.
    key: str
    # The token only this holder knows: 40 hexadecimal digits of 20 random
    # bytes.
    token: str
    # The time to live it was taken for, and `renew` extends it by, in
    # whole milliseconds.
    ttl: time.Duration
    # The fencing number: it grows with every acquisition of the key, so
    # the resource refuses a holder whose number is below the highest it
    # has seen (the module's comment).
    fence: i64

# Takes the lock `key` where nobody holds it, for `ttl`, trying once.
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `key`: the key that holds the lock; the key `key` followed by `:fence`
#   counts its acquisitions.
# - `ttl`: how long after which the lock is gone unless `renew` extends
#   it, 1 ms or more, a part of a millisecond counting as a whole one:
#   longer than the work it guards, by a margin.
#
# Fails `exists` where another holds it, `invalid` where `ttl` is below 1
# ms, and as `call` does; `failed` where the system gives no random bytes
# for the token. See also `lock`, `unlock`, and the module's comment for
# what a lock does not promise.
function try_lock(@c: Client, key: str, ttl: time.Duration) -> Lock?
    ttl_ms = session.whole_ms(ttl)

    if ttl_ms < 1
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the time to live {time.format_duration(ttl)} is below 1 millisecond")
    token = bytes.format_hex(os.random_bytes(20)?)
    r = call(@c, ["EVAL", lock_scripts.ACQUIRE, "2", key, key + ":fence", token, to_str(ttl_ms)])?

    if is_null(r) || r == .boolean(v: false)
        return fail(code: EXISTS, msg: f"the lock \"{key}\" is held by another")
    return ok(Lock(key: key, token: token, ttl: ttl, fence: int_of(r)?))

# Takes the lock `key`, trying again until `wait` has passed, each wait after a refusal longer than the one before, from 10 to
# 250 milliseconds, with a random part so two waiters do not try together.
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `key`: the key that holds the lock.
# - `ttl`: as `try_lock` takes it.
# - `wait`: how long to keep trying, zero or more; zero tries once.
#
# Fails `timeout` where another holds the lock all that time, `invalid`
# where `wait` is below zero, and as `try_lock` does. See also `try_lock`.
function lock(@c: Client, key: str, ttl: time.Duration, wait: time.Duration) -> Lock?
    if wait.nanoseconds < 0
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the wait {time.format_duration(wait)} is below zero")
    wait_ms = session.whole_ms(wait)
    start = time.mark()?
    pause: i64 @= 10

    while true
        match try_lock(@c, key, ttl: ttl)
            .ok held => return ok(held)
            .err e =>
                if e.code != EXISTS
                    return fail(code: e.code, msg: e.msg)

        waited = time.to_units(time.elapsed(start)?, unit: .millisecond)

        if waited >= wait_ms
            return fail(
                code: TIMEOUT,
                msg: f"the lock \"{key}\" was held by another for the {time.format_duration(wait)} waited"
            )
        jitter = to_i64(os.random_bytes(1)?[0]).must() % (pause / 2 + 1)
        left = wait_ms - waited

        nap = if pause + jitter < left
            pause + jitter
        else
            left
        time.sleep(time.milliseconds(nap)?)

        pause @ if pause * 2 < 250
            pause * 2
        else
            250

# Gives `held` back: deletes its key, only where it still holds this
# holder's token, by a script the server runs whole.
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `held`: the lock, from `try_lock` or `lock`.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the lock had expired, so another may hold it now,
# and as `call` does. See also `renew`.
function unlock(@c: Client, held: Lock) -> ()?
    r = call(@c, ["EVAL", lock_scripts.RELEASE, "1", held.key, held.token])?

    if int_of(r)? == 0
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"the lock \"{held.key}\" had expired and is no longer this holder's")
    return ok()

# Extends `held` by its time to live, from now, only where it still holds
# this holder's token.
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `held`: the lock.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the lock had expired, and as `call` does. See also
# `unlock`.
function renew(@c: Client, held: Lock) -> ()?
    r = call(@c, ["EVAL", lock_scripts.RENEW, "1", held.key, held.token, to_str(session.whole_ms(held.ttl))])?

    if int_of(r)? == 0
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"the lock \"{held.key}\" had expired and is no longer this holder's")
    return ok()

# Whether `held`'s key still holds this holder's token: true until it
# expires or is given back.
#
# - `c`: the client.
# - `held`: the lock.
#
# The answer may be stale the moment it comes. Fails as `call` does. See
# also `renew`.
function is_held(@c: Client, held: Lock) -> bool?
    r = call(@c, ["GET", held.key])?

    if is_null(r)
        return ok(false)
    return ok(text_of(r)? == held.token)

## Tests

test "decode: each type of the documentation, RESP2's nulls, a map, nested arrays and an attribute dropped"
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("+OK\r\n")).must() == .simple(v: "OK")
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("-WRONGTYPE Operation against a key\r\n")).must() == .error(code: "WRONGTYPE", message: "Operation against a key")
    assert decode(bytes.from_text(":-7\r\n")).must() == .integer(v: -7)
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("$5\r\nhello\r\n")).must() == .bulk(v: bytes.from_text("hello"))
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("$-1\r\n")).must() == .null_value
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("*-1\r\n")).must() == .null_value
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("*0\r\n")).must() == .array(items: [])
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("_\r\n")).must() == .null_value
    assert decode(bytes.from_text(",-inf\r\n")).must() == .double(v: -1.0 / 0.0)
    assert decode(bytes.from_text(",1.5e3\r\n")).must() == .double(v: 1500.0)
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("#f\r\n")).must() == .boolean(v: false)
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("(-3492890328409238509324850943850943825024385\r\n")).must() == .big_number(v: "-3492890328409238509324850943850943825024385")
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("!21\r\nSYNTAX invalid syntax\r\n")).must() == .error(code: "SYNTAX", message: "invalid syntax")
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("=15\r\ntxt:Some string\r\n")).must() == .verbatim(format: "txt", v: "Some string")
    pairs = decode(bytes.from_text("%2\r\n+first\r\n:1\r\n+second\r\n:2\r\n")).must()
    assert pairs == .mapping(entries: [Entry(key: .simple(v: "first"), value: .integer(v: 1)), Entry(key: .simple(v: "second"), value: .integer(v: 2))])
    nested = decode(bytes.from_text("*2\r\n*3\r\n:1\r\n:2\r\n:3\r\n*2\r\n+Hello\r\n-World\r\n")).must()
    assert nested == .array(items: [.array(items: [.integer(v: 1), .integer(v: 2), .integer(v: 3)]), .array(items: [.simple(v: "Hello"), .error(code: "", message: "World")])])
    noted = decode(
        bytes.from_text("|1\r\n+key-popularity\r\n%2\r\n$1\r\na\r\n,0.1923\r\n$1\r\nb\r\n,0.0012\r\n*2\r\n:2039123\r\n:9543892\r\n")
    ).must()
    assert noted == .array(items: [.integer(v: 2039123), .integer(v: 9543892)])
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("~2\r\n+a\r\n+b\r\n")).must() == .set(items: [.simple(v: "a"), .simple(v: "b")])
    assert decode(bytes.from_text(">3\r\n+message\r\n+ch\r\n+hi\r\n")).must() == .pushed(items: [.simple(v: "message"), .simple(v: "ch"), .simple(v: "hi")])

test "decode and decode_next: bytes after a reply, a reply cut short, nesting past the bound, and two replies in turn"
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("+OK\r\n+OK\r\n")).is_err()
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("*2\r\n:1\r\n")).is_err()
    assert decode(bytes.from_text(",1.2.3\r\n")).is_err()
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("(12a\r\n")).is_err()
    deep: [u8] @= []

    for _ in range(from: 0, to: 129)
        session.append(out: @deep, data: bytes.from_text("*1\r\n"))

    session.append(out: @deep, data: bytes.from_text(":1\r\n"))

    match decode(deep)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "invalid" && e.msg == "the reply nests past 128 levels"

    two = bytes.from_text(":1\r\n$2\r\nhi\r\n")
    first = decode_next(two, from: 0).must()
    assert first.reply == .integer(v: 1) && first.next == 4
    assert decode_next(two, from: first.next).must().reply == .bulk(v: bytes.from_text("hi"))

test "decode: an array of a hundred thousand elements, read in time linear in its length"
    big: [u8] @= bytes.from_text("*100000\r\n")

    for _ in range(from: 0, to: 100000)
        session.append(out: @big, data: bytes.from_text(":7\r\n"))

    got = list_of(decode(big).must()).must()
    assert got.len() == 100000 && got[99999] == .integer(v: 7)

test "readers: text, bytes, integers and floats from either protocol, nulls not_found, errors told with their code"
    assert text_of(.bulk(v: bytes.from_text("città"))).must() == "città"
    assert text_of(.bulk(v: [255])).is_err()
    assert bytes_of(.bulk(v: [255])).must() == [255]
    assert int_of(.bulk(v: bytes.from_text("42"))).must() == 42
    assert float_of(.bulk(v: bytes.from_text("inf"))).must() == 1.0 / 0.0
    assert float_of(.integer(v: 3)).must() == 3.0
    assert bool_of(.integer(v: 1)).must() && !bool_of(.boolean(v: false)).must()
    assert bool_of(.integer(v: 2)).is_err()

    match text_of(.null_value)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "not_found"

    match int_of(.error(code: "WRONGTYPE", message: "Operation against a key holding the wrong kind of value"))
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "invalid" && e.msg == "[WRONGTYPE] Operation against a key holding the wrong kind of value" && error_code_of(e.msg).must() == "WRONGTYPE"

    assert texts_of(.array(items: [.bulk(v: bytes.from_text("a")), .simple(v: "b")])).must() == ["a", "b"]
    assert texts_of(.array(items: [.null_value])).is_err()
    flat = entries_of(.array(items: [.bulk(v: bytes.from_text("f")), .bulk(v: bytes.from_text("1"))])).must()
    assert flat == [Entry(key: .bulk(v: bytes.from_text("f")), value: .bulk(v: bytes.from_text("1")))]
    assert entries_of(.array(items: [.integer(v: 1)])).is_err()
    assert error_code_of("no word").is_err()

test "call: the bytes of a command, its reply, an error told with its code, and a reply cut short ends the client"
    t @= server.scripted("+OK\r\n-WRONGTYPE Operation against a key holding the wrong kind of value\r\n:3", protocol: 2)
    c @= Client(session: t.session)
    assert call(@c, ["SET", "k", "v w"]).must() == .simple(v: "OK")

    match call(@c, ["LPUSH", "k", "x"])
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "invalid" && error_code_of(e.msg).must() == "WRONGTYPE"

    match call(@c, ["INCR", "n"])
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "closed"

    assert is_closed(c)
    assert call(@c, []).is_err()
    t.session @ c.session
    written = server.finish(@t)
    assert written == server.command(["SET", "k", "v w"]) + server.command(["LPUSH", "k", "x"]) + server.command(["INCR", "n"])

test "pipeline and transaction: replies in order with an error in its place, a transaction run, aborted by a watch, and refused when queued"
    t @= server.scripted(
        "+OK\r\n-ERR value is not an integer or out of range\r\n$1\r\n1\r\n+OK\r\n+QUEUED\r\n+QUEUED\r\n*2\r\n:1\r\n:2\r\n+OK\r\n+QUEUED\r\n_\r\n+OK\r\n-ERR unknown command 'NOPE'\r\n-EXECABORT Transaction discarded because of previous errors.\r\n",
        protocol: 3
    )
    c @= Client(session: t.session)
    replies = pipeline(@c, [["SET", "a", "1"], ["INCR", "s"], ["GET", "a"]]).must()
    assert replies.len() == 3 && replies[0] == .simple(v: "OK") && checked(replies[1]).is_err() && text_of(replies[2]).must() == "1"
    done = transaction(@c, [["INCR", "a"], ["INCR", "b"]]).must()
    assert done == Transaction(committed: true, replies: [.integer(v: 1), .integer(v: 2)])
    aborted = transaction(@c, [["INCR", "a"]]).must()
    assert !aborted.committed && aborted.replies == []

    match transaction(@c, [["NOPE"]])
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "unsupported" && e.msg == "[ERR] unknown command 'NOPE'"

    t.session @ c.session
    written = server.finish(@t)
    assert written == server.command(["SET", "a", "1"]) + server.command(["INCR", "s"]) + server.command(["GET", "a"]) + server.command(["MULTI"]) + server.command(["INCR", "a"]) + server.command(["INCR", "b"]) + server.command(["EXEC"]) + server.command(["MULTI"]) + server.command(["INCR", "a"]) + server.command(["EXEC"]) + server.command(["MULTI"]) + server.command(["NOPE"]) + server.command(["EXEC"])

test "subscribe and receive over RESP2: confirmations counted, a message before the last kept, a pattern's message, and commands refused while subscribed"
    t @= server.scripted(
        "*3\r\n$9\r\nsubscribe\r\n$1\r\na\r\n:1\r\n*3\r\n$7\r\nmessage\r\n$1\r\na\r\n$2\r\nhi\r\n*3\r\n$9\r\nsubscribe\r\n$1\r\nb\r\n:2\r\n*4\r\n$8\r\npmessage\r\n$2\r\nb*\r\n$2\r\nbe\r\n$1\r\nx\r\n*3\r\n$11\r\nunsubscribe\r\n$1\r\na\r\n:1\r\n*3\r\n$11\r\nunsubscribe\r\n$1\r\nb\r\n:0\r\n",
        protocol: 2
    )
    c @= Client(session: t.session)
    subscribe(@c, ["a", "b"]).must()
    assert c.session.channels == ["a", "b"]

    match call(@c, ["GET", "k"])
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "invalid"

    assert receive(@c).must() == Message(kind: "message", pattern: "", channel: "a", payload: bytes.from_text("hi"))
    assert receive(@c).must() == Message(kind: "pmessage", pattern: "b*", channel: "be", payload: bytes.from_text("x"))
    unsubscribe(@c, []).must()
    assert c.session.channels == []

    match receive(@c)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "invalid"

    t.session @ c.session
    written = server.finish(@t)
    assert written == server.command(["SUBSCRIBE", "a", "b"]) + server.command(["UNSUBSCRIBE"])

test "read_reply over RESP3: a push that comes before a reply is kept for receive"
    t @= server.scripted(">3\r\n$7\r\nmessage\r\n$2\r\nch\r\n$3\r\nyes\r\n:5\r\n", protocol: 3)
    c @= Client(session: t.session)
    c.session.channels @ ["ch"]
    assert publish(@c, channel: "other", message: "x").must() == 5
    assert receive(@c).must() == Message(kind: "message", pattern: "", channel: "ch", payload: bytes.from_text("yes"))
    t.session @ c.session
    _ = server.finish(@t)

test "try_lock, unlock and renew: the scripts sent, a lock taken, held by another, lost, given back"
    t @= server.scripted(":1\r\n_\r\n:1\r\n:0\r\n:0\r\n", protocol: 3)
    c @= Client(session: t.session)
    held = try_lock(@c, "job", ttl: time.seconds(30).must()).must()
    assert held.key == "job" && held.token.len() == 40 && held.fence == 1 && held.ttl == time.seconds(30).must()

    match try_lock(@c, "job", ttl: time.seconds(30).must())
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "exists"

    renew(@c, held).must()

    match renew(@c, held)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "not_found"

    match unlock(@c, held)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "not_found"

    assert try_lock(@c, "job", ttl: time.nanoseconds(0)).is_err()
    t.session @ c.session
    written = server.finish(@t)
    assert strings.has_prefix(
        s: written,
        prefix: server.command(["EVAL", lock_scripts.ACQUIRE, "2", "job", "job:fence", held.token, "30000"])
    )
    assert strings.contains(s: written, substr: server.command(["EVAL", lock_scripts.RELEASE, "1", "job", held.token]))

test "parse_url: plain and TLS, a password alone, a database, the protocol, and the refusals"
    plain = parse_url("redis://localhost:6380/2").must()
    assert plain.port == 6380 && plain.database == 2 && plain.security == .plain && plain.protocol == 3
    tls_url = parse_url("rediss://ana:s%3Acret@cache.lan?protocol=2&client_name=app").must()
    assert tls_url.security == .checked(roots: "") && tls_url.username == "ana" && tls_url.password == "s:cret" && tls_url.protocol == 2 && tls_url.client_name == "app"
    alone = parse_url("redis://:pw@h").must()
    assert alone.username == "" && alone.password == "pw" && alone.host == "h"
    assert parse_url("redis://h/x").is_err()
    assert parse_url("redis://h?protocol=4").is_err()
    assert parse_url("redis://h?timeout=1").is_err()
    assert parse_url("http://h").is_err()

    match parse_url("unix:///tmp/redis.sock")
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "unsupported"

test "connect: a port, a database and a protocol out of range, refused before connecting"
    bad @= options("localhost")
    bad.port @ 0
    assert connect(bad).is_err()
    bad.port @ 6379
    bad.database @ -1
    assert connect(bad).is_err()
    bad.database @ 0
    bad.protocol @ 4
    assert connect(bad).is_err()

test "connect live, against the server REBEL_REDIS_URL names: both protocols, values, pipelines, transactions, a watch, messages, locks and failures"
    match os.env("REBEL_REDIS_URL")
        .err _ => skip_test(reason: "REBEL_REDIS_URL names no server")
        .ok text =>
            opts = parse_url(text).must()
            c @= connect(opts).must()
            assert protocol_of(c) == 3
            two_opts @= opts
            two_opts.protocol @ 2
            old @= connect(two_opts).must()
            assert protocol_of(old) == 2
            assert text_of(call(@c, ["PING"]).must()).must() == "PONG"
            set(@c, key: "live:s", value: "città 🎸").must()
            assert get(@c, "live:s").must() == "città 🎸" && get(@old, "live:s").must() == "città 🎸"

            match get(@c, "live:none")
                .ok _  => assert false
                .err e => assert e.code == "not_found"

            _ = call_bytes(@c, [bytes.from_text("SET"), bytes.from_text("live:b"), [0, 255, 13, 10]]).must()
            assert bytes_of(call(@old, ["GET", "live:b"]).must()).must() == [0, 255, 13, 10]
            _ = delete(@c, ["live:n", "live:h", "live:z", "live:l", "live:w"]).must()
            assert increment(@c, "live:n", by: 5).must() == 5 && increment(@old, "live:n", by: -7).must() == -2
            _ = call(@c, ["HSET", "live:h", "f1", "1", "f2", "2"]).must()
            three = entries_of(call(@c, ["HGETALL", "live:h"]).must()).must()
            two = entries_of(call(@old, ["HGETALL", "live:h"]).must()).must()
            assert three.len() == 2 && two.len() == 2 && text_of(three[0].key).must() == text_of(two[0].key).must()
            _ = call(@c, ["ZADD", "live:z", "1.5", "a"]).must()
            assert float_of(call(@c, ["ZSCORE", "live:z", "a"]).must()).must() == 1.5
            assert float_of(call(@old, ["ZSCORE", "live:z", "a"]).must()).must() == 1.5
            assert bool_of(call(@c, ["EXISTS", "live:z"]).must()).must()
            set_for(@c, key: "live:t", value: "x", ttl: time.minutes(1).must()).must()
            assert int_of(call(@c, ["PTTL", "live:t"]).must()).must() > 0
            big: [str] @= ["RPUSH", "live:l"]

            for i in range(from: 0, to: 10000)
                big @ big.push(to_str(i))

            assert int_of(call(@c, big).must()).must() == 10000
            assert texts_of(call(@c, ["LRANGE", "live:l", "0", "-1"]).must()).must().len() == 10000
            large = repeat("é", 1000000)
            set(@c, key: "live:large", value: large).must()
            assert get(@old, "live:large").must() == large
            many: [[str]] @= []

            for _ in range(from: 0, to: 1000)
                many @ many.push(["INCR", "live:p"])

            replies = pipeline(@c, many).must()
            assert replies.len() == 1000 && int_of(replies[999]).must() == int_of(replies[0]).must() + 999
            done = transaction(@c, [["SET", "live:w", "1"], ["INCR", "live:w"], ["LPUSH", "live:w", "x"]]).must()
            assert done.committed && done.replies.len() == 3 && checked(done.replies[2]).is_err()
            watch(@c, ["live:w"]).must()
            set(@old, key: "live:w", value: "changed").must()
            aborted = transaction(@c, [["SET", "live:w", "mine"]]).must()
            assert !aborted.committed && get(@c, "live:w").must() == "changed"

            match call(@c, ["LPUSH", "live:s", "x"])
                .ok _  => assert false
                .err e => assert e.code == "invalid" && error_code_of(e.msg).must() == "WRONGTYPE"

            match call(@c, ["NOSUCHCOMMAND"])
                .ok _  => assert false
                .err e => assert e.code == "unsupported"

            listener @= connect(opts).must()
            old_listener @= connect(two_opts).must()
            subscribe(@listener, ["live-chat"]).must()
            psubscribe(@old_listener, ["live-*"]).must()
            assert publish(@c, channel: "live-chat", message: "hi").must() == 2
            assert receive(@listener).must() == Message(kind: "message", pattern: "", channel: "live-chat", payload: bytes.from_text("hi"))
            assert receive(@old_listener).must() == Message(kind: "pmessage", pattern: "live-*", channel: "live-chat", payload: bytes.from_text("hi"))
            assert text_of(call(@listener, ["GET", "live:s"]).must()).must() == "città 🎸"
            set_deadline(listener, deadline: time.milliseconds(200).must()).must()

            match receive(@listener)
                .ok _  => assert false
                .err e => assert e.code == "timeout"

            close(@listener).must()
            punsubscribe(@old_listener, []).must()
            close(@old_listener).must()
            _ = delete(@c, ["live:lock"]).must()
            first = try_lock(@c, "live:lock", ttl: time.milliseconds(300).must()).must()
            assert is_held(@c, first).must()

            match try_lock(@old, "live:lock", ttl: time.milliseconds(300).must())
                .ok _  => assert false
                .err e => assert e.code == "exists"

            renew(@c, first).must()
            second = lock(@old, "live:lock", ttl: time.seconds(5).must(), wait: time.seconds(3).must()).must()
            assert second.fence == first.fence + 1 && !is_held(@c, first).must()

            match unlock(@c, first)
                .ok _  => assert false
                .err e => assert e.code == "not_found"

            unlock(@old, second).must()
            assert !is_held(@old, second).must()
            close(@old).must()
            wrong @= opts
            wrong.password @ "not the password"

            user = if opts.username == ""
                "default"
            else
                opts.username

            # The server is asked first, on the connection already open,
            # whether it refuses this user a wrong password: one started with
            # no password, or whose user is `nopass`, answers OK to any, and a
            # connection with a wrong one proves nothing there.
            match call(@c, ["AUTH", user, wrong.password])
                .ok _ => print(
                    "connect live: a wrong password not tried, since the server takes any password for this user"
                )
                .err asked =>
                    if asked.code == "permission"
                        match connect(wrong)
                            .ok _  => assert false
                            .err e => assert e.code == "permission"
                    else
                        print(
                            f"connect live: a wrong password not tried, since the server answered AUTH with {asked.code}"
                        )

            close(@c).must()
            print(
                f"connect live: RESP3 and RESP2, 10000 elements, 2 MB, 1000 in a pipeline, a watch, messages and a lock fenced {second.fence}"
            )

test "connect live over TLS, against the server REBEL_REDIS_TLS_PORT names on localhost, its certificate signed by tls's test authority"
    match os.env("REBEL_REDIS_TLS_PORT")
        .err _ => skip_test(reason: "REBEL_REDIS_TLS_PORT names no server")
        .ok port =>
            opts @= options("localhost")
            opts.port @ numbers.parse_int(port).must()
            opts.password @ os.env("REBEL_REDIS_PASSWORD").default("")
            opts.security @ .checked(roots: tls.TEST_ROOT)
            c @= connect(opts).must()
            assert text_of(call(@c, ["PING"]).must()).must() == "PONG"
            print(
                f"connect live over TLS: RESP{protocol_of(c)} over TLS version {tls.version_of(c.session.out).default(0)}"
            )
            close(@c).must()

## Examples

# The options of a server on this machine, which serves no TLS, as Redis by
# default.
test "options: a local server in plain text"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain
    assert opts.port == 6379 && opts.protocol == 3

# The options a URL names.
test "parse_url: a password, a host and a database"
    opts = parse_url("redis://:s3cret@cache.lan:6380/1").must()
    assert opts.password == "s3cret" && opts.host == "cache.lan" && opts.port == 6380 && opts.database == 1

# A client opened and closed, where a server answers on this machine in
# plain text; elsewhere it skips.
test "connect: a connection to a local server"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            assert !is_closed(c)
            close(@c).must()

# A client closed, after which every call fails `closed`.
test "close: the end of a connection"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            close(@c).must()
            assert call(@c, ["PING"]).is_err()

# Whether a client is still open.
test "is_closed: before and after close"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            assert !is_closed(c)
            close(@c).must()
            assert is_closed(c)

# The protocol a local server agreed.
test "protocol_of: RESP3 where the server speaks it"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            assert protocol_of(c) == 2 || protocol_of(c) == 3
            close(@c).must()

# A wait for a message bounded to a tenth of a second.
test "set_deadline: a receive that times out"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            subscribe(@c, ["rebel-quiet"]).must()
            set_deadline(c, deadline: time.milliseconds(100).must()).must()
            assert receive(@c).is_err()
            close(@c).must()

# A reply read from its bytes.
test "decode: a bulk string"
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("$5\r\nhello\r\n")).must() == .bulk(v: bytes.from_text("hello"))

# Two replies read one after the other.
test "decode_next: an integer then a simple string"
    data = bytes.from_text(":1\r\n+OK\r\n")
    first = decode_next(data, from: 0).must()
    assert decode_next(data, from: first.next).must().reply == .simple(v: "OK")

# The bytes of a command.
test "encode_command: GET of a key"
    assert encode_command([bytes.from_text("GET"), bytes.from_text("k")]) == bytes.from_text("*2\r\n$3\r\nGET\r\n$1\r\nk\r\n")

# An error reply turned into a failure.
test "checked: an error and a reply that is none"
    assert checked(.simple(v: "OK")).must() == .simple(v: "OK")
    assert checked(.error(code: "ERR", message: "syntax error")).is_err()

# The word of an error's failure.
test "error_code_of: the word of a WRONGTYPE"
    assert error_code_of("[WRONGTYPE] Operation against a key holding the wrong kind of value").must() == "WRONGTYPE"

# What a reply is, as a message names it.
test "kind_of: a map"
    assert kind_of(.mapping(entries: [])) == "a map"

# A null reply.
test "is_null: GET of a key not there"
    assert is_null(.null_value) && !is_null(.integer(v: 0))

# Text read from a bulk string.
test "text_of: a bulk string's text"
    assert text_of(.bulk(v: bytes.from_text("hello"))).must() == "hello"

# Bytes read from a bulk string.
test "bytes_of: bytes that are not text"
    assert bytes_of(.bulk(v: [0, 255])).must() == [0, 255]

# An integer read from RESP2's string of digits.
test "int_of: a counter read by HGET"
    assert int_of(.bulk(v: bytes.from_text("12"))).must() == 12

# A score read from a double.
test "float_of: a score"
    assert float_of(.double(v: 1.5)).must() == 1.5

# A truth read from RESP2's integer.
test "bool_of: EXISTS of a key"
    assert bool_of(.integer(v: 1)).must()

# The elements of an array.
test "list_of: an array of two"
    assert list_of(.array(items: [.integer(v: 1), .integer(v: 2)])).must().len() == 2

# The texts of an array.
test "texts_of: the members of a set"
    assert texts_of(.set(items: [.simple(v: "a")])).must() == ["a"]

# The entries of a map.
test "entries_of: a field and its value"
    got = entries_of(.mapping(entries: [Entry(key: .simple(v: "f"), value: .integer(v: 1))])).must()
    assert got[0].value == .integer(v: 1)

# A command and its reply, written and read by hand.
test "send: a PING written"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            send(@c, [bytes.from_text("PING")]).must()
            assert text_of(read_reply(@c).must()).must() == "PONG"
            close(@c).must()

# The reply to a command written by `send`.
test "read_reply: the answer of an ECHO"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            send(@c, [bytes.from_text("ECHO"), bytes.from_text("hi")]).must()
            assert text_of(read_reply(@c).must()).must() == "hi"
            close(@c).must()

# A command run.
test "call: SET then GET"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            _ = call(@c, ["SET", "rebel:example", "Ziggy"]).must()
            assert text_of(call(@c, ["GET", "rebel:example"]).must()).must() == "Ziggy"
            close(@c).must()

# A command whose argument is bytes.
test "call_bytes: a value that is not text"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            _ = call_bytes(@c, [bytes.from_text("SET"), bytes.from_text("rebel:raw"), [0, 255]]).must()
            assert bytes_of(call(@c, ["GET", "rebel:raw"]).must()).must() == [0, 255]
            close(@c).must()

# A key read.
test "get: a key's text"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            set(@c, key: "rebel:get", value: "x").must()
            assert get(@c, "rebel:get").must() == "x"
            close(@c).must()

# A key written.
test "set: a key's text"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            set(@c, key: "rebel:set", value: "y").must()
            close(@c).must()

# A key written for a minute.
test "set_for: a key that expires"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            set_for(@c, key: "rebel:session", value: "z", ttl: time.minutes(1).must()).must()
            close(@c).must()

# Keys deleted.
test "delete: a key and one not there"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            set(@c, key: "rebel:gone", value: "1").must()
            assert delete(@c, ["rebel:gone", "rebel:never"]).must() == 1
            close(@c).must()

# A counter moved.
test "increment: a counter moved by five"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            _ = delete(@c, ["rebel:count"]).must()
            assert increment(@c, "rebel:count", by: 5).must() == 5
            close(@c).must()

# Three commands in one round trip.
test "pipeline: three commands"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            replies = pipeline(@c, [["SET", "rebel:p", "1"], ["INCR", "rebel:p"], ["GET", "rebel:p"]]).must()
            assert text_of(replies[2]).must() == "2"
            close(@c).must()

# Two commands run as one.
test "transaction: two counters moved together"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            done = transaction(@c, [["INCR", "rebel:a"], ["INCR", "rebel:b"]]).must()
            assert done.committed && done.replies.len() == 2
            close(@c).must()

# A key watched before a transaction.
test "watch: a check-and-set"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            watch(@c, ["rebel:balance"]).must()
            done = transaction(@c, [["INCRBY", "rebel:balance", "10"]]).must()
            assert done.committed
            close(@c).must()

# Keys watched, then forgotten.
test "unwatch: a watch given up"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            watch(@c, ["rebel:balance"]).must()
            unwatch(@c).must()
            close(@c).must()

# A message posted to a channel.
test "publish: a message nobody hears"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            assert publish(@c, channel: "rebel-nobody", message: "hello").must() >= 0
            close(@c).must()

# A channel subscribed to, then left.
test "subscribe: a channel"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            subscribe(@c, ["rebel-news"]).must()
            unsubscribe(@c, []).must()
            close(@c).must()

# Channels subscribed to by a pattern.
test "psubscribe: every channel of a family"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            psubscribe(@c, ["rebel-news.*"]).must()
            punsubscribe(@c, []).must()
            close(@c).must()

# A subscription ended.
test "unsubscribe: every channel"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            subscribe(@c, ["rebel-a", "rebel-b"]).must()
            unsubscribe(@c, ["rebel-a"]).must()
            unsubscribe(@c, []).must()
            close(@c).must()

# A pattern's subscription ended.
test "punsubscribe: one pattern"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            psubscribe(@c, ["rebel-x.*"]).must()
            punsubscribe(@c, ["rebel-x.*"]).must()
            close(@c).must()

# A message received from another client's publish.
test "receive: a message published by another client"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            listener @= first
            speaker @= connect(opts).must()
            subscribe(@listener, ["rebel-chat"]).must()
            _ = publish(@speaker, channel: "rebel-chat", message: "hi").must()
            assert receive(@listener).must().payload == bytes.from_text("hi")
            close(@speaker).must()
            close(@listener).must()

# The message of a push.
test "message_of: a push of a channel's message"
    m = message_of(
        .pushed(items: [.bulk(v: bytes.from_text("message")), .bulk(v: bytes.from_text("ch")), .bulk(v: bytes.from_text("hi"))])
    ).must()
    assert m.channel == "ch" && m.payload == bytes.from_text("hi")

# A lock taken once and given back.
test "try_lock: a job run by one worker"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first

            match try_lock(@c, "rebel:job", ttl: time.seconds(30).must())
                .err _   => _ = 0
                .ok held => unlock(@c, held).must()

            close(@c).must()

# A lock waited for.
test "lock: waiting a second at most"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            held = lock(@c, "rebel:report", ttl: time.seconds(10).must(), wait: time.seconds(1).must()).must()
            unlock(@c, held).must()
            close(@c).must()

# A lock given back.
test "unlock: the end of the work"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            held = lock(@c, "rebel:unlock", ttl: time.seconds(10).must(), wait: time.seconds(1).must()).must()
            unlock(@c, held).must()
            assert !is_held(@c, held).must()
            close(@c).must()

# A lock extended while the work goes on.
test "renew: a lock kept"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            held = lock(@c, "rebel:renew", ttl: time.seconds(10).must(), wait: time.seconds(1).must()).must()
            renew(@c, held).must()
            unlock(@c, held).must()
            close(@c).must()

# Whether a lock is still held.
test "is_held: a lock before and after it is given back"
    opts @= options("localhost")
    opts.security @ .plain

    match connect(opts)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no Redis server reached on this machine: {e.msg}")
        .ok first =>
            c @= first
            held = lock(@c, "rebel:held", ttl: time.seconds(10).must(), wait: time.seconds(1).must()).must()
            assert is_held(@c, held).must()
            unlock(@c, held).must()
            close(@c).must()
